the hydrogen Club has been a dog  training facility located in downtown  Las Vegas for the last nine years but  the owner says she can no longer  compromise safety to continue the vision  that entrepreneur Tony Shea once had for  this area nine years ago today  I stood right here  and announced the opening of this park  it's a full circle for hydrogen club  owner Kathy Brooks hi Buster he said  Brooks on delay entrepreneur Tony Shay  met in 2012 when they decided to open  the private dog boarding and training  facility as part of the downtown project  this was a dream  that I didn't know that I had  that  the blessing that was Tony Shea giving  the downtown area a new vision from  crime and grime to thriving businesses  he would walk by and there would be this  Oasis that gave everyone a sense of calm  and peace in the middle of Fremont  Street but Brooke said recently the  homeless have taken over making it  nearly impossible to get through her day  safely I've had rocks thrown at my head  I've been spat on I had someone throw a  drink at the fence at me but the last  straw was during an overnight shift at  the facility at about 10 10 15 I'm awake  into the sound of gunfire right outside  the wall against which my head is  resting just this weekend someone broke  off their doorknob I have clients who  stopped coming down here they're like I  don't feel safe downtown anymore good  boy is It ultimately Brooks wasn't sure  she was safe anymore which meant she  couldn't jeopardize the safety of the  dogs the hydrant Club will close its  doors on December 31st until then it  will remain open every single day so its  members can continue playing and say  goodbye to their friends  Victoria Saha 8 News Now
 

